PASS5.push(
 {t:'The Night Garden',p:['Every evening, when the city lights blinked on, Mr. Okafor climbed to the roof of his apartment building with a watering can.','Up there, between the chimneys, he had planted moonflowers, white blossoms that open only after dark.','His neighbors thought he was strange until one hot July night, when the power went out.','Families climbed to the roof to escape the heat and found the flowers glowing like small lanterns.','By the end of the summer, the roof had six new gardeners.'],
  q:[['Why did the neighbors change their minds about Mr. Okafor?','They saw how beautiful his night garden was when they came to the roof.',['He gave them money.','The building made a new rule.','He stopped gardening.'],'What happened on the hot July night?','When the power went out, they <b>saw the glowing garden</b> for themselves.'],
     ['What does the simile "glowing like small lanterns" help the reader picture?','white flowers shining in the dark',['lanterns falling from the sky','a fire on the roof','the power coming back'],'A simile compares two things using like or as.','The flowers looked like <b>little lights in the dark</b>.'],
     ['Which theme does the story suggest?','Something that seems strange at first can bring people together.',['People should not change their minds.','Neighbors should mind their own business.','Gardens should only grow in daylight.'],'Think about the six new gardeners.','The "strange" garden <b>brought the neighbors together</b>.']]},
 {t:'How a Glacier Moves',p:['A glacier is a huge mass of ice that forms where more snow falls each winter than melts each summer.','Over many years, the snow is pressed down into thick ice.','When the ice becomes heavy enough, gravity pulls it slowly downhill, sometimes only a few inches a day.','As it moves, a glacier scrapes rocks and soil, carving deep, U-shaped valleys.','Yosemite Valley in California was shaped this way thousands of years ago.'],
  q:[['What causes a glacier to move?','Gravity pulls the heavy ice downhill.',['Wind pushes it.','Ocean tides pull it.','Earthquakes shake it loose.'],'Look at the third sentence.','<b>Gravity</b> pulls the heavy ice.'],
     ['How is this text mostly organized?','cause and effect, in the order things happen',['problem and solution','comparing two glaciers','a list of opinions'],'Does it tell steps and what each one causes?','It explains, step by step, <b>what causes what</b>.'],
     ['Why does the author mention Yosemite Valley?','to give a real example of land shaped by a glacier',['to tell readers to visit','to compare two parks','to describe the weather there'],'What does the example prove?','It is a <b>real example</b> of a glacier-carved valley.']]},
 {t:'Two Views of the School Garden (two texts)',p:['<b>Text 1, a news article:</b> Lincoln Elementary opened a garden last spring. Students planted tomatoes, beans and squash. The school says students now spend 30 minutes a week outdoors learning about plants.','<b>Text 2, a student\'s journal:</b> At first, I hated getting dirt under my nails. But when my first tomato turned red, I felt so proud that I took a picture and showed my whole family.'],
  q:[['How are the two texts different?','Text 1 gives facts; Text 2 shares one student\'s feelings.',['Both share only feelings.','Text 2 gives more numbers.','Text 1 is a poem.'],'Compare what each one focuses on.','Text 1 reports <b>facts</b>; Text 2 shows <b>a personal experience</b>.'],
     ['What information appears in Text 1 but not Text 2?','how much time students spend in the garden each week',['how the student felt about the tomato','that the student showed her family','that dirt got under nails'],'Look for a number.','Only Text 1 says <b>30 minutes a week</b>.'],
     ['How did the student\'s feelings change in Text 2?','from disliking the garden to feeling proud',['from proud to bored','from happy to angry','they did not change'],'Compare the beginning and the end.','She went from <b>hating the dirt</b> to <b>feeling proud</b>.']]},
 {t:'Morning on the Lake (a poem)',lines:true,p:['The lake lies still, a silver plate,','where herons stand and patiently wait.','The mist rolls back like a sleepy sheet,','and frogs begin their morning beat.','','A fisherman rows with a gentle oar,','and ripples run to the farthest shore.'],
  q:[['What is the lake compared to in the first line?','a silver plate',['a sleepy sheet','a gentle oar','a morning beat'],'Look at line 1.','The lake is called <b>a silver plate</b>: a metaphor.'],
     ['What is the mood of the poem?','calm and peaceful',['scary and dark','angry and loud','sad and lonely'],'Think about the words still, patiently and gentle.','The words create a <b>calm, peaceful</b> mood.'],
     ['How is the poem organized?','in stanzas with rhyming pairs of lines',['as one long paragraph','as a list of facts','as a conversation'],'Look at the line endings and the blank line.','Two <b>stanzas</b> with <b>rhyming pairs</b>.']]}
);
