LISTEN5.push(
 {t:'How Volcanoes Form Islands',txt:'Did you know that the Hawaiian Islands were built by volcanoes? Deep under the Pacific Ocean, there is a hot spot where melted rock rises from inside Earth. Each time a volcano erupts there, lava cools and hardens into new rock. Over hundreds of thousands of years, the rock piles higher and higher until it rises above the water as an island. Because the ocean floor slowly moves, new islands form in a line, one after another. The youngest island, the Big Island, still has active volcanoes today.',
  q:[['What is the speaker\'s main point?','Volcanoes over a hot spot built the Hawaiian Islands over a very long time.',['Hawaii is a nice place to visit.','All islands are made of sand.','Volcanoes only happen on land.'],'Listen for the big idea in the first sentence.','Volcanoes <b>built the islands</b> over a long time.'],
     ['Why do the islands form in a line?','The ocean floor slowly moves over the hot spot.',['Each volcano pushes the next one away.','The wind blows them.','Lava flows in one direction only.'],'Listen for the word "because".','<b>The ocean floor moves</b>, so new islands form in a line.'],
     ['Which detail supports the idea that the Big Island is the youngest?','It still has active volcanoes.',['It is the biggest.','It has the most people.','It is the farthest north.'],'Listen to the last sentence.','Its volcanoes are <b>still active</b>.']]},
 {t:'Class President Debate',txt:'Good afternoon. My name is Aisha, and I believe our school needs more water fountains. Right now, there are only two fountains for four hundred students, so lines at recess are long, and many kids go back to class thirsty. Last month, I counted the line every day: it averaged twelve students. If I am elected, I will ask the principal to add two refill stations, which also cut down on plastic bottles. A healthy school is a hydrated school. Thank you.',
  q:[['What is Aisha\'s main claim?','The school needs more water fountains.',['Recess should be longer.','Plastic bottles are good.','The principal is wrong.'],'Listen for "I believe".','She believes the school <b>needs more water fountains</b>.'],
     ['Which evidence does Aisha give?','She counted an average of twelve students in line each day.',['She says water tastes good.','She says her friends agree.','She says the fountains are old.'],'Listen for numbers.','She <b>counted the line every day</b>: about twelve students.'],
     ['Which reason does Aisha give for refill stations besides less waiting?','They cut down on plastic bottles.',['They are cheaper than fountains.','They keep water colder.','They take up less space.'],'Listen for "which also".','Refill stations <b>cut down on plastic bottles</b>.']]},
 {t:'The Story of the Golden Spike',txt:'On May tenth, eighteen sixty-nine, two railroad companies met at Promontory Summit in Utah. The Central Pacific had built east from California, and the Union Pacific had built west from Nebraska. Workers, including thousands of Chinese and Irish immigrants, had laid tracks across mountains and deserts. A golden spike was tapped into the final tie, and a telegraph message of one word, "Done," was sent across the country. Now a trip from coast to coast took about a week instead of months.',
  q:[['Where did the two railroads meet?','Promontory Summit, Utah',['San Francisco, California','Omaha, Nebraska','New York City'],'Listen to the first sentence.','They met at <b>Promontory Summit, Utah</b>.'],
     ['Who did much of the work building the railroad?','thousands of Chinese and Irish immigrants',['only soldiers','mostly farmers from the East','mostly gold miners'],'Listen for who laid the tracks.','<b>Chinese and Irish immigrants</b> did much of the work.'],
     ['How did the railroad change travel?','A coast-to-coast trip took about a week instead of months.',['Travel became slower.','People stopped traveling.','A trip took about a month instead of a year.'],'Listen to the last sentence.','The trip took <b>about a week instead of months</b>.']]}
);
